  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=x?$",
  "worked_solution": "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(-2,-1),(-2,3),$ and $(2,1).$\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-2,-1)}=-2, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-2,3)}=-2, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(2,1)}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040080/assets/74cf2bd34be1693b5f0ce93303ac0039058a5fda17023dd94836563ea9d0404a.png)",
